/**
\mainpage File System Wrapper

The EFI filesystem driver presents files through EFI_FILE_PROTOCOL. The shared
reader translates those requests into operations on a mounted volume, a named
filesystem node and an open stream. For an ordinary file read, the sequence is
fsw_efi_file_read(), fsw_shandle_read(), the backend's get_extent callback and,
for physical data, fsw_block_get(). Directory reads also use a stream, but its
position can be a backend-specific iteration cookie.

\section identity Volumes, node identity and ownership

A backend declares volume_struct_size and dnode_struct_size in its
fsw_fstype_table. The core allocates the declared size. A backend-specific
structure embeds the common structure first so both dispatch layers can use
the same allocation. The volume owns the physical block cache and indexes its
live nodes. Host-specific state is reached through the volume's host_data.

Within a mounted volume the live-node key is the pair of 64-bit values
(tree_id, dnode_id). This matters on Btrfs, where distinct subvolumes can
reuse an inode number. fsw_dnode_create_with_tree() retains an existing match.
A newly created node retains its parent. fsw_dnode_release() on the final
reference unlinks the node, frees its backend-specific data and name, then
releases the parent. Releasing a child can therefore release several ancestors.

fsw_shandle_open() retains one node reference per open stream. Opening the same
file twice shares the node and volume cache but creates independent positions
and cached extent mappings. fsw_shandle_close() frees any extent buffer owned
by that stream and drops its node reference.

\section extents From a file offset to bytes

A file position and its length are measured in bytes. An extent's log_start and
log_count are in logical filesystem blocks. A physical extent's phys_start is
in the volume's physical blocks. The backend supplies both block sizes when it
mounts. fsw_set_blocksize() discards old cache entries when those units change.

- A physical extent maps a contiguous run. The reader pins one physical block,
  copies up to its boundary, then unpins it. Cache storage remains with the volume.
- A buffered extent transfers allocated data to the stream on success. A
  backend can use this for inline or compressed data. The stream frees it before
  replacing the mapping or when it closes.
- A sparse extent describes a hole. The reader fills the corresponding slice
  of the destination with zeroes.

\subsection split_read A read across a block boundary

Consider a file of 4110 bytes with 4096-byte logical and physical blocks.
Its extent has log_start 0, log_count 2 and phys_start 90. A request for
20 bytes at file offset 4090 proceeds as follows.

- Offset 4090 lies six bytes before the end of physical block 90. Copy its
  bytes 4090 through 4095 and release the pin.
- Offset 4096 lies in the same extent, at the start of physical block 91.
  Copy that block's first 14 bytes. No second extent lookup is needed.
- The reader commits a returned size of 20 and a new stream position of 4110.
  A subsequent read at that position succeeds with a returned size of zero.

A 20-byte request starting at 4106 instead returns four bytes. If block 91
fails during the original request, the first six destination bytes may already
have changed. The function returns the I/O error with the stream still at 4090
and the caller's count still at 20. A caller must check the status before
using the count or the destination.

The stream's working byte position now retains fsw_u64 precision throughout
fsw_shandle_read(), so a seek at or beyond 4 GiB is no longer truncated in
the core. A single request's byte count is still fsw_u32; the EFI adapter
narrows UINTN before calling the core. High-offset correctness also depends
on the filesystem backend's extent mapping and has not been qualified with
independently generated large-file fixtures. Position width and request-size
width are separate contracts.

\section paths Names and directory cursors

An fsw_string carries its encoding, logical length, byte size and data pointer.
A three-code-unit UTF-16 name has len 3 and size 6. A path component returned by
fsw_strsplit() borrows its input. fsw_strdup_coerce() creates owned, counted
storage. For identical encodings it copies exactly size bytes, so callers
cannot assume a trailing NUL.

fsw_efi_dir_open() borrows the caller's CHAR16 path during lookup, resolves
the final symbolic link and returns an EFI handle that retains the resulting
node through a new stream. Intermediate node references can be released once
the next layer has acquired its own.

At end of directory the backend returns FSW_NOT_FOUND. fsw_efi_dir_read()
translates that result to EFI_SUCCESS with a size of zero. With a live child,
the backend advances its cursor before the adapter measures EFI_FILE_INFO.
The EFI adapter now saves the prior cursor and restores it when conversion
fails, including EFI_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL, after releasing the child dnode.
A retry of the same handle therefore re-examines the same directory entry.
A firmware regression still needs to demonstrate the behaviour across
multiple entries and successively sized buffers.
*/
